-- Q1: hard / L4 — Two-class method: participating preferred undistributed earnings allocation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stern Industries has net income of $3,000,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares and 100,000 shares of participating preferred stock entitled to share ratably in all dividends with common shareholders. No dividends were declared during the year. The CFO proposes computing basic EPS as $6.00 ($3,000,000 / 500,000) by ignoring the preferred since no dividends were declared. Evaluate whether this approach is correct.', '["The approach overstates EPS; undistributed earnings must be allocated proportionally, yielding basic EPS of $5.50", "The approach is correct; participating preferred shares are included only when dividends are declared", "The approach overstates EPS; undistributed earnings must be allocated to participating preferred, yielding basic EPS of $5.00", "The approach understates EPS; the preferred shares reduce the denominator rather than the numerator"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method requires allocating undistributed earnings to all participating securities based on their contractual participation rights. Undistributed earnings of $3,000,000 are allocated proportionally: common receives $3,000,000 x (500,000/600,000) = $2,500,000, yielding basic EPS of $5.00. Choice B is incorrect because the two-class method applies regardless of whether dividends are declared; participation rights obligate allocation of all undistributed earnings.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — Control number: loss from continuing operations makes all securities antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redfield Corp. reports a loss from continuing operations of $200,000 and a gain from discontinued operations of $500,000, for net income of $300,000. The company has 100,000 common shares and 10,000 dilutive stock options. Analyze how the loss from continuing operations affects the diluted EPS calculation for the discontinued operations line item.', '["The options are included in diluted EPS for discontinued operations because net income is positive", "The options are excluded from diluted EPS for all line items because income from continuing operations is the control number", "The options are included only in the continuing operations EPS calculation", "The options are excluded from continuing operations EPS but included in discontinued operations EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations serves as the control number for determining whether securities are dilutive. Because Redfield has a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS for every line item presented, including discontinued operations. Choice A is incorrect because net income is not the control number; the standard specifically designates income from continuing operations.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred mid-year conversion diluted EPS calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Corp. has net income of $720,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares. On January 1, 10,000 shares of $100 par, 6% cumulative preferred stock were outstanding, each convertible into 5 common shares. On July 1, all preferred shares converted. What is diluted EPS?', '["$2.76", "$3.07", "$3.20", "$2.88"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the beginning of the period. For preferred stock that converted on July 1, diluted EPS assumes conversion on January 1, eliminating the $30,000 preferred dividend deduction and including all 50,000 converted shares for the full year. Diluted EPS = $720,000 / 250,000 = $2.88. Choice B ($3.07) is incorrect because it reflects basic EPS, which includes converted shares only from the July 1 conversion date rather than assuming full-year conversion.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Share buyback effect on basic EPS trend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Clearwater Industries reports net income of $1,500,000 with 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1. On April 1, the company repurchases 60,000 shares. Analyze the effect of the repurchase on basic EPS compared to what EPS would have been without the buyback.', '["Basic EPS increases from $5.00 to $5.88 because the buyback reduces weighted-average shares", "Basic EPS decreases because the cash used for the buyback reduces net income", "Basic EPS remains $5.00 because repurchased shares are excluded only after year-end", "Basic EPS increases from $5.00 to $6.25 because the repurchased shares are removed for the full year"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, repurchased shares are excluded from the weighted-average from the repurchase date. Weighted-average shares = (300,000 x 3/12) + (240,000 x 9/12) = 255,000, yielding EPS of $5.88 versus $5.00 without the buyback. Choice D ($6.25) incorrectly removes the 60,000 shares for the entire year rather than only from the April 1 repurchase date forward.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Simple vs. complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which characteristic distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["The entity has more than one class of common stock", "The entity has issued both short-term and long-term debt", "The entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute EPS", "The entity has reported other comprehensive income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding potentially dilutive securities such as stock options, warrants, or convertible instruments. A simple capital structure has only common stock and no potentially dilutive securities. Choice A is incorrect because multiple classes of common stock alone do not create a complex capital structure; the key factor is the existence of dilutive potential shares.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Premium on convertible bonds: effective interest vs. coupon in if-converted numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Garfield Electronics issued $1,000,000 face value, 8% convertible bonds at 105. Annual interest expense using the effective interest method is $74,000 and the tax rate is 25%. An analyst computing diluted EPS adds back $80,000 (the coupon payment) rather than $74,000 to the numerator. Analyze the error in the analyst''s approach.', '["The analyst should add back $60,000, using the coupon amount multiplied by the tax rate", "The analyst should add back $55,500, using the effective interest expense net of tax", "The analyst should add back $80,000 but without any tax adjustment", "The analyst should add back $74,000 without any tax adjustment"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back interest expense net of tax. The correct adjustment uses the effective interest expense of $74,000 (not the $80,000 coupon) multiplied by (1 - 0.25): $74,000 x 0.75 = $55,500. Choice D is incorrect because the interest savings must be tax-affected; eliminating interest expense also eliminates the associated tax deduction, so only the after-tax amount increases net income.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Written put options on own shares: reverse treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonebridge Corp. has net income of $2,400,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares. The company wrote put options obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares at $60 per share when the average market price is $45. The controller excludes the put options from diluted EPS because the exercise price exceeds the market price. Evaluate this treatment.', '["Correct; written puts are excluded when the exercise price exceeds the market price", "Incorrect; the put options add 20,000 shares directly to the denominator, yielding diluted EPS of $5.71", "Correct; written puts are always excluded from diluted EPS calculations", "Incorrect; written puts are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the market price, yielding diluted EPS of $5.90"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options use the reverse treasury stock method. When the exercise price ($60) exceeds the average market price ($45), the entity would need to repurchase $1,200,000 / $45 = 26,667 shares at market to satisfy the 20,000-share obligation, adding 6,667 incremental shares. Diluted EPS = $2,400,000 / 406,667 = $5.90. Choice A is incorrect because written puts follow the opposite dilution logic from call options.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Multiple share transactions weighted-average calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Hawthorne Corp. had the following common share activity during 2025: 150,000 shares on January 1; 30,000 issued on March 1; 10,000 repurchased on August 1; and 20,000 issued on November 1. What is the weighted-average number of shares for 2025?', '["174,167", "190,000", "170,000", "180,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average is computed by weighting each period: (150,000 x 2/12) + (180,000 x 5/12) + (170,000 x 3/12) + (190,000 x 2/12) = 25,000 + 75,000 + 42,500 + 31,667 = 174,167. Choice B (190,000) is incorrect because it uses the year-end balance rather than weighting each period by the fraction of the year those shares were outstanding.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — EPS presentation with discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. reports income from continuing operations of $480,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $120,000 (net of tax), for net income of $360,000. Weighted-average shares are 120,000 with no preferred dividends. What per-share amounts must Meridian present?', '["Only net income EPS of $3.00 must be presented on the income statement", "EPS of $4.00 for continuing operations and $3.00 for net income, with discontinued operations in notes only", "EPS of $4.00 for continuing operations, ($1.00) for discontinued operations, and $3.00 for net income", "Only $4.00 EPS for continuing operations is required because it is the control number"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS for discontinued operations must also be presented either on the face of the statement or in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 requires separate EPS presentation for continuing operations; net income EPS alone is insufficient.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under U.S. GAAP", "Public entities with common stock or potential common stock traded on a public market", "Only entities with complex capital structures", "Only entities that have declared dividends during the period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for public business entities that have issued common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market. Private companies are not required to present EPS, though they may elect to do so. Choice A is incorrect because nonpublic entities have no obligation to report EPS under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Warrants under treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Manufacturing has 250,000 weighted-average common shares, net income of $875,000, and 15,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $30. The average market price during 2025 is $50. Using the treasury stock method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.50", "$3.30", "$3.60", "$3.42"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, warrants use the treasury stock method identically to stock options. The 15,000 warrants generate $450,000 in proceeds, buying back 9,000 shares at the $50 average market price, for 6,000 incremental shares. Diluted EPS = $875,000 / 256,000 = $3.42. Choice A ($3.50) is incorrect because it uses only the 250,000 base shares rather than including the 6,000 incremental dilutive shares from the warrants.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, the treasury stock method for stock options assumes that exercise proceeds are used to:', '["Repurchase common shares at the average market price during the period", "Repurchase common shares at the year-end closing market price", "Retire outstanding debt to reduce interest expense", "Repurchase common shares at the exercise price"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-23, the treasury stock method assumes option exercise proceeds are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price during the period. Only the net incremental shares are added to the denominator. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 specifies the average market price, not the year-end price, to avoid giving undue weight to a single point-in-time measurement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — RSUs in diluted EPS using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Larkspur Inc. has net income of $600,000 and 150,000 weighted-average shares. The company granted 12,000 restricted stock units (RSUs) with service-based vesting. The average unrecognized compensation cost is $18 per RSU, and the average market price during 2025 is $60. How many incremental shares are added for diluted EPS?', '["12,000", "3,600", "8,400", "6,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28A, unvested RSUs are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the unamortized compensation cost ($18 x 12,000 = $216,000), used to repurchase $216,000 / $60 = 3,600 shares. Incremental shares are 12,000 - 3,600 = 8,400. Choice A (12,000) is incorrect because it adds all RSUs to the denominator rather than netting against the assumed share repurchase from unrecognized compensation cost.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — Mandatorily convertible instruments: basic vs. diluted EPS treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Financial Group has $500,000 of mandatorily convertible preferred stock (8% dividend) that must convert into 40,000 common shares on January 1, 2027. During 2025, $40,000 in preferred dividends were paid. The CFO includes the 40,000 shares in basic EPS for 2025 because conversion is mandatory. Evaluate whether this treatment complies with ASC 260.', '["Correct; mandatory conversion means the shares should be included in basic EPS from the issuance date", "Incorrect; mandatorily convertible shares are excluded from basic EPS until the conversion date but included in diluted EPS via the if-converted method", "Correct; mandatory conversion creates a binding obligation equivalent to outstanding common stock", "Incorrect; mandatorily convertible shares are excluded from both basic and diluted EPS until the actual conversion date"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-14, mandatorily convertible instruments are not included in weighted-average common shares for basic EPS until the actual conversion date. They are treated as potentially dilutive securities and included in diluted EPS using the if-converted method. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 260 distinguishes between outstanding common stock and instruments that will become common stock; the latter enter basic EPS only upon conversion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Control number definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the "control number" used to determine whether potentially dilutive securities are included in diluted EPS?', '["Net income for the period", "Total comprehensive income", "Operating income for the period", "Income from continuing operations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income from continuing operations is the control number for determining dilution. If a loss from continuing operations exists, all potentially dilutive securities are considered antidilutive and excluded, even if net income is positive due to discontinued operations. Choice A is incorrect because net income may include gains from discontinued operations that could mask a continuing-operations loss.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Post-period stock split retroactive EPS adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Foxworth Corp. reports basic EPS of $4.50 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on 200,000 weighted-average shares. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, the board declares a 2-for-1 stock split. Analyze how this post-period stock split affects the 2025 EPS presentation.', '["The 2025 EPS must be retroactively adjusted to $2.25 based on 400,000 post-split shares", "The 2025 EPS remains $4.50 with a footnote disclosing the subsequent stock split", "The 2025 EPS is adjusted only if the split is effective before the SEC filing deadline", "The 2025 EPS remains $4.50 because the split occurs in a subsequent fiscal period"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be given retroactive effect in the EPS computation. The 2025 weighted-average shares are adjusted to 400,000, and basic EPS is restated to $2.25. Choice B is incorrect because disclosure alone is insufficient; ASC 260 requires retroactive adjustment of all EPS data presented to reflect the split.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Two-class method basic EPS calculation
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Holdings has net income of $900,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares. The company also has 50,000 shares of Class B preferred stock that participates equally in dividends with common shareholders. Dividends of $250,000 were declared ($200,000 to common, $50,000 to preferred). What is basic EPS under the two-class method?', '["$4.50", "$3.25", "$3.60", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates both distributed and undistributed earnings. Distributed earnings to common: $200,000. Undistributed: $650,000 x (200,000/250,000) = $520,000 to common. Basic EPS = ($200,000 + $520,000) / 200,000 = $3.60. Choice A ($4.50) is incorrect because it computes $900,000 / 200,000 without allocating undistributed earnings to the participating preferred, as required by the two-class method.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Tax adjustment error in if-converted numerator
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Briarwood Inc. has $500,000 of 10% convertible bonds and a 30% effective tax rate. The controller adds back $50,000 (the full coupon interest) to the diluted EPS numerator without tax-adjusting it. Analyze the effect of this error on diluted EPS.', '["The error understates diluted EPS because the numerator should be higher by the tax savings", "The error overstates diluted EPS because the after-tax interest savings is only $35,000, not $50,000", "There is no error because the if-converted method uses pre-tax interest", "The error understates diluted EPS because the denominator should also be increased"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back interest net of tax because eliminating interest also eliminates the related tax deduction. The correct add-back is $50,000 x (1 - 0.30) = $35,000. Adding $50,000 overstates the numerator by $15,000, inflating diluted EPS. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires a tax adjustment to the interest add-back in the numerator.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Contingently issuable shares definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, contingently issuable shares are defined as shares that are:', '["Issuable upon satisfaction of specified conditions such as reaching a performance target", "Held in treasury and available for reissuance at management''s discretion", "Reserved for stock option exercises under an employee benefit plan", "Issuable upon conversion of outstanding convertible preferred stock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, contingently issuable shares are shares issuable for little or no cash consideration upon the satisfaction of specified conditions such as earnings targets or share price thresholds. Choice D is incorrect because convertible preferred stock represents a conversion right, not a contingent issuance; conversion depends on the holder''s election rather than meeting an external condition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L3 — Rights issue with bonus element requires EPS adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pemberton Inc. announces a rights offering allowing one new share for every four held at $25 per share. Before the offering, 400,000 shares are outstanding at a market price of $50. The theoretical ex-rights price is $45. Analyze how this rights offering affects the weighted-average shares for EPS purposes.', '["No adjustment is needed because rights offerings are not stock dividends", "Pre-rights weighted-average shares are reduced by the ratio of exercise price to market price", "Only the new shares are added to the weighted-average from the issuance date without further adjustment", "Pre-rights weighted-average shares are multiplied by an adjustment factor of 1.111 to reflect the bonus element"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-14 through 55-17, a rights issue priced below market contains a bonus element. The adjustment factor equals the fair value before the rights divided by the theoretical ex-rights price: $50 / $45 = 1.111. Pre-rights weighted-average shares are multiplied by this factor to ensure comparability. Choice C is incorrect because it ignores the bonus element embedded in the below-market offering price.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Multiple dilutive securities: correct ordering by per-share impact
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ridgeline Corp. has net income of $2,000,000 and 500,000 weighted-average shares (basic EPS $4.00). Three securities are outstanding: (1) 20,000 stock options adding 8,000 incremental shares, (2) $400,000 of 5% convertible bonds converting to 15,000 shares (tax rate 25%), and (3) 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred converting to 12,000 shares. Evaluate the correct inclusion order and resulting diluted EPS.', '["Options ($0.00), then bonds ($1.00), then preferred ($3.33), yielding diluted EPS of $3.84", "Preferred ($3.33), then bonds ($1.00), then options ($0.00), yielding diluted EPS of $3.84", "All three included simultaneously without ranking, yielding diluted EPS of $3.84", "Options first, then preferred, then bonds, yielding diluted EPS of $3.73"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities must be ranked from most dilutive (lowest per-share impact) to least dilutive and tested sequentially. Options rank first ($0/8,000 = $0.00), bonds second ($15,000/15,000 = $1.00), and preferred third ($40,000/12,000 = $3.33). Each is included only if it reduces cumulative EPS. Choice B is incorrect because testing from least dilutive to most dilutive could incorrectly include antidilutive securities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — EPS must appear on face of income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, where must a public entity present its earnings per share data?', '["In the notes to the financial statements only", "On the statement of stockholders'' equity", "On the face of the income statement", "In the management discussion and analysis section"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities must present both basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. Choice A is incorrect because while additional EPS disclosures may appear in the notes, the primary EPS figures must be presented on the income statement itself.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: easy / L2 — Shares issued as compensation: weighted-average
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Edgewater Corp. had 100,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On October 1, 2025, the company issued 8,000 shares to employees as stock compensation. What is the weighted-average number of shares for basic EPS in 2025?', '["108,000", "102,000", "100,000", "104,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, newly issued shares are included in the weighted-average from the date of issuance. The 8,000 shares issued on October 1 are weighted for three months: (100,000 x 9/12) + (108,000 x 3/12) = 75,000 + 27,000 = 102,000. Choice A (108,000) is incorrect because it uses the year-end count rather than weighting the new shares for the three months they were outstanding.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Contracts settleable in cash or shares: presumption of more dilutive method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Holdings enters a forward contract to sell 50,000 of its own shares at $40 per share in six months. The average market price during the reporting period is $35. The contract may be settled in cash or shares at Northgate''s election. The controller excludes the contract from diluted EPS because the entity intends to settle in cash. Evaluate this treatment under ASC 260.', '["Correct; the entity''s intent to settle in cash permits exclusion from diluted EPS", "Incorrect; the contract must always be assumed settled in shares, adding 50,000 to the denominator", "Correct; forward sale contracts are excluded from diluted EPS under ASC 260", "Incorrect; ASC 260 requires using the more dilutive settlement assumption regardless of stated intent"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-45, contracts that may be settled in stock or cash must assume the more dilutive method of settlement when computing diluted EPS, regardless of the entity''s stated intent. The presumption of share settlement is rebuttable only if past experience or stated policy supports cash settlement and the entity has the ability. Choice A is incorrect because mere intent to settle in cash does not automatically exclude contracts from diluted EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Convertible bonds issued at discount: diluted EPS with effective interest
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornfield Corp. issued $800,000 face value, 6% convertible bonds at 95. Annual effective interest expense is $52,000. Each $1,000 bond converts into 25 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. Net income is $1,300,000 with 400,000 weighted-average shares. What is diluted EPS?', '["$3.19", "$3.25", "$3.34", "$3.11"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method adds back after-tax interest expense and adds conversion shares to the denominator. After-tax interest = $52,000 x 0.75 = $39,000. Conversion adds 800 bonds x 25 = 20,000 shares. Diluted EPS = ($1,300,000 + $39,000) / (400,000 + 20,000) = $1,339,000 / 420,000 = $3.19. Choice B ($3.25) is incorrect because it represents basic EPS rather than incorporating the dilutive effect of the convertible bonds into the computation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Performance-based options: incremental shares when conditions met
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redwood Industries has 300,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $1,050,000. The company granted 18,000 performance-based stock options with an exercise price of $35. As of December 31, 2025, the performance conditions are met, and the average market price during 2025 is $70. How many incremental shares are added for diluted EPS?', '["18,000 incremental shares", "9,000 incremental shares", "Zero — performance options are always excluded", "5,000 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-28, performance-based options whose conditions are met as of the reporting date are included in diluted EPS using the treasury stock method. Proceeds of $630,000 (18,000 x $35) repurchase 9,000 shares at the $70 average market price, yielding 9,000 incremental shares. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 requires inclusion of contingently issuable options when performance conditions have been satisfied by period end.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Tax rate change effect on if-converted numerator adjustment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Maple Ridge Corp. has $1,000,000 of 7% convertible bonds. In 2025, the statutory tax rate decreased from 30% to 21%. An analyst computes the diluted EPS numerator adjustment as $49,000 ($70,000 x 0.70, using the old 30% rate). Analyze the effect of using the incorrect tax rate on diluted EPS.', '["The numerator is overstated by $6,300, making diluted EPS appear higher", "There is no effect because the tax rate does not apply to the if-converted adjustment", "The numerator is understated by $6,300, making diluted EPS appear lower than correct", "The numerator is understated by $9,000, making diluted EPS significantly lower"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted interest add-back must use the current period''s effective tax rate. At 21%, the correct after-tax add-back is $70,000 x 0.79 = $55,300, not $49,000. Using the old 30% rate understates the numerator by $6,300, producing a lower diluted EPS than is correct. Choice A is incorrect because it reverses the direction of the error; a smaller numerator produces lower EPS rather than higher EPS.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — ASC 260 as governing standard
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which ASC topic governs the computation and presentation of earnings per share under U.S. GAAP?', '["ASC 220", "ASC 250", "ASC 280", "ASC 260"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ASC 260, Earnings Per Share, is the authoritative guidance governing the computation, presentation, and disclosure of EPS for public business entities. While Choice A (ASC 220) addresses comprehensive income, Choice B (ASC 250) covers accounting changes, and Choice C (ASC 280) addresses segment reporting, none of these govern EPS computation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Nonpublic entity EPS exemption
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Which type of entity is exempt from presenting earnings per share under ASC 260?', '["A privately held company whose stock is not traded on a public market", "A public company with only a single class of common stock", "A public company that has declared no dividends during the period", "A public company with a simple capital structure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, only public business entities with common stock or potential common stock traded in a public market are required to present EPS. Privately held companies are exempt from this requirement. Choice B is incorrect because all public entities must present EPS regardless of how many classes of stock they have outstanding.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: medium / L3 — Options exercised mid-year: basic vs. diluted denominator treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Silverton Corp. had 200,000 common shares on January 1, 2025. On July 1, employees exercised 10,000 stock options (exercise price $25, average market price $50 for January through June). Analyze the difference in how these exercised options affect the basic versus diluted EPS denominators.', '["Both denominators include the 10,000 shares from January 1 because exercise is assumed at the period start", "The diluted denominator is 207,500 because it includes 2,500 incremental shares from the pre-exercise period via the treasury stock method", "The diluted denominator equals the basic denominator of 205,000 because exercised options are no longer potentially dilutive", "The diluted denominator is 210,000 because the full 10,000 shares are included for the entire year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-26, options exercised during the period are included in diluted EPS via the treasury stock method from the beginning of the period to the exercise date, and as actual outstanding shares from the exercise date forward. The treasury stock method adds 5,000 incremental shares for six months (2,500 weighted), yielding a diluted denominator of 207,500. Choice C is incorrect because the pre-exercise dilutive effect must still be captured in diluted EPS.', 'medium', 3)
;

COMMIT;
